rstand very well how it gives us the  forest and why  and then we think we're gonna get a  little section of the forest but we go  immediately all the way down to a leaf  and some insect on it we don't manage to  get to the sort of the structure we're  interested in because we don't have the  sort of dial to move around these things  so i don't think it's incoherent in a  statement but i do think um what you  want is the ability to move smoothly  across the spectra and you kind of want  that across all sciences and it relates  very closely to this just you know i  think that those spectra is kind of like  between physics and biology and things  like that  yeah um and i think um we will let's  close with that um  uh very very nice uh question and i  think you know i'm just very tempted to  make one small comment is that you know  often they have been sort of uh you know  the sociologists of science have often  critiqued narratives of science that  scientists offer in which they separate  out the context and the context doesn't  really matter because of  this sort of you know um slim uh notion  and understanding of objectivity and and  i think that the context really matters  and i think both neil and sarah are  telling us that the kinds of questions  you ask and what question you ask really  depends on the context and what you know  what is it that informs the question is  almost as important as the question  itself  right  so um thanks everyone for this wonderful  session and i thank you both and um see  you all at um the next uh next month's  colloquium bye take care and thank you  thank you
 

